The value of $\tan A$ will be maximum when $BC$ is perpendicular to $\overline{AC}$</think>
Answer:
[asy]
import olympiad;
size(5cm);
defaultpen(linewidth(0.7)+fontsize(11pt));
pair A=origin, B=(15,0), C=(15,20), P=foot(C,B,A);
draw (A--B--C--cycle);
draw(rightanglemark(C,P,B,0.8)); draw(rightanglemark(P,A,C,0.8));
label("A", A, SW);
label("B", B, S); label("$\overline{AC}$",midpoint(A--C), NW);
label("$\overline{BC}$", B+ (-0.9,-0.3));
label("$\overline{AB}$",  A+ (0.4,0.2));
markscalefactor=0.1;
draw(rightanglemark(B,P,C,1.3));
dot(A^^B^^C);
label("15",midpoint(A--C), W); label("20",midpoint(C--B), NW);
[/asy]

$BC$ is perpendicular to $\overline{AC}$ when $BC$ is a perpendicular from $C$ to the side $AB.$  By the definition of $\tan,$ this means $\tan A$ is at its maximum.  It is easy to check that $BC \perp \overline{AC}$ because that would imply $BC = 15$ and $\triangle ABC$ is isosceles.  $\tan A = BC/AC = \boxed{4/3}.$

